        "block": false
    },
    {
        "text": "As she sits out in the field, racking her brains, Titi falls into slumber. She dreams, a dream where she is but seven or eight."
    },
    {
        "text": "Her father was already ill at the time. With one cold hand, he held her, while with the other, he flipped through the tome atop his knee, slowly reciting from it."
    },
    {
        "text": "He never bought her children's books, or chose books specifically for her. He simply went with what he liked, but it didn't matter, because Titi liked those stories too."
    },
    {
        "text": "Within those tales, the silent hero Siopi does not speak a word for thirty years, all to keep a secret. Kereseira, refusing to perform for outlanders, hangs her harp atop a laurel tree."
    },
    {
        "text": "Perhaps it is the filter of dreams at work, but her father seems to tower over her, the slightly stooped back of her memories now ramrod-straight, his voice one of undeniable confidence."
    },
    {
        "text": "'All of us have the chance to become a hero. When your duty falls upon you, you need only take it up with courage, Titi.'"
    },
    {
        "text": "Really? Can I? How can you be so sure?"
    },
    {
        "text": "It has been so long since she last saw her father, and she wants to ask him these questions, but it is only a dream, and it refuses to bend to her will. In the end, she could not ask what she wished to ask."
    },
    {
        "text": "The gentle light of the twin moons falls upon the pasture, and upon the girl."
    },
    {
        "text": "The land is painted in silver. A breeze blows, curling up the girl's ribbons, lifting the burdenbeasts' headscarves."
    },
    {
        "text": "Even the antiquated, marred harp takes on a gentle radiance under the moonlight."
    },
    {
        "text": "A teardrop slowly falls from the corner of Titi's eye. It rolls into the bushes, soon gone from sight."
    },
    {
